# DD(1)

## NAME

dd - convert and copy a file

## SYNOPSIS

| **dd** [option=value] ...

## DESCRIPTION

*Dd* copies the specified input file to the specified output with possible conversions. The
standard input and output are used by default. The input and output block size may be specified
to take advantage of raw physical I/O.

*option*
: | *values*

if=
: | input file name; standard input is default

of=
: | output file name; standard output is default

ibs=*n*
: | input block size *n* bytes (default 3072)

obs=*n*
: | output block size (default 3072)

bs=*n*
: | set both input and output block size, superseding *ibs* and *obs;* also, if no conversion is specified, it is particularly efficient since no copy need be done

cbs=*n*
: | conversion buffer size

skip=*n*
: | skip *n* input records before starting copy

files=*n*
: | copy *n* files from (tape) input

seek=*n*
: | seek *n* records from beginning of output file before copying

count=*n*
: | copy only *n* input records

conv=ascii
: | convert EBCDIC to ASCII

ebcdic
: | convert ASCII to EBCDIC

ibm
: | slightly different map of ASCII to EBCDIC

lcase
: | map alphabetics to lower case

ucase
: | map alphabetics to upper case

swab
: | swap every pair of bytes

noerror
: | do not stop processing on an error

sync
: | pad every input record to *ibs*

... , ...
: several comma-separated conversions

Where sizes are specified, a number of bytes is expected. A number may end with **k, b** or
**w** to specify multiplication by 1024, 3072, or 6 respectively; a pair of numbers may be
separated by **x** to indicate a product.

**Note:** two of those three multipliers differ from v7's, which were 512 and 2. Here **b** is
this machine's filesystem block, 3072 bytes, and **w** is one 48-bit *word*, six bytes - the
unit **w** was always meant to name. So **1b**, **3k** and **512w** are all the same number,
and the default *ibs* and *obs* above are one of them. A **w** here is three times a PDP-11's,
so a v7 script asking for **512w** gets 3072 bytes rather than 1024.

*Cbs* is used only if *ascii* or *ebcdic* conversion is specified. In the former case *cbs*
characters are placed into the conversion buffer, converted to ASCII, and trailing blanks
trimmed and new-line added before sending the line to the output. In the latter case ASCII
characters are read into the conversion buffer, converted to EBCDIC, and blanks added to make
up an output record of size *cbs*.

After completion, *dd* reports the number of whole and partial input and output blocks.

For example, to read a file of EBCDIC card images blocked ten 80-byte images per record into
the ASCII file *x*:

> dd if=cards of=x ibs=800 cbs=80 conv=ascii,lcase

**Note:** v7 gave this example with *if=/dev/rmt0* and a second one reading past a file mark on
the same device. Both are gone: there is no magnetic tape on this machine, no tape driver in
the kernel and no *cdevsw* row for one. The conversion tables are still here, so the example is
kept with an ordinary file as its input.

## RAW DEVICES

**Note:** this section is new, and it is the reason *dd* was ported before *mkfs*(1M) and
*fsck*(1M).

*Dd* is the only program here that can move bulk data to or from */dev/rmd0* and */dev/rmb0*,
and a transfer on either is not the ordinary read(2) it looks like: it goes through *physio*()
and the disk driver, which between them accept only **whole blocks** of 3072 bytes. So *ibs*
and *obs* must be multiples of 3072 whenever a device is named - which the defaults above
already are, so the common cases need no *bs* at all.

Give a size that is not, and the transfer is **refused** rather than truncated. Two different
sizes fail two different ways, and the diagnostic says which: a size that is not a whole number
of 48-bit words earns **Bad address,** and one that is a whole number of words but not a whole
block earns **I/O error.**

**Note:** the offset needs no such care, and that is a property of this program rather than of
the device. Every offset *dd* can produce is a multiple of *obs* - *seek* gives *n*\**obs*, and
each record written advances by *obs* - so if the record size is a whole number of blocks then
every offset is block-aligned too, and if it is not, the very first transfer fails on the size
before any offset is used. A misaligned offset is the one condition the kernel does **not**
diagnose, and *dd* cannot reach it.

**Note:** the raw disk has **no end of file.** A read past the last block of the volume is an
**I/O error,** not a count of zero, so a copy *from* the device must say how much to copy with
*count*.

**Note:** a copy *to* the device fails on its last record if the input is not a whole number of
blocks, there being no way to write part of one. *conv=sync* pads the short record out to *ibs*
and is what that case wants.

## SUPER-USER ONLY

**Note:** */dev/rmd0* is mode 0600 because that one node is the contents of every file on the
volume, so any use of *dd* on it works for the super-user and for nobody else. *Dd* is
deliberately **not** set-user-id, and the reason is stronger than the one *df*(1M) gives for
itself: a set-uid *df* would let anyone read the whole filesystem, and a set-uid *dd* would let
them rewrite it.

## SEE ALSO

cp(1), tr(1), df(1M)

## DIAGNOSTICS

f+p records in(out): numbers of full and partial records read(written)

**Note:** *dd* exits 1 when it fails - on an unrecognised argument, on a file it cannot open or
create, on a zero *ibs* or *obs*, and on a read or write error it was not told to ignore. v7
printed every one of those diagnostics and then exited **0**, so no shell script could tell a
refused copy from a completed one. An interrupt still exits 0, as in v7: it is not a failure of
the program, and the records copied up to that point are reported as usual.

## BUGS

The ASCII/EBCDIC conversion tables are taken from the 256 character standard in the CACM Nov,
1968\. The `ibm` conversion, while less blessed as a standard, corresponds better to certain IBM
print train conventions. There is no universal solution.

Newlines are inserted only on conversion to ASCII; padding is done only on conversion to
EBCDIC. These should be separate options.

**Note:** four more, all v7's and all carried rather than fixed, because a fix for any of them
would be a behaviour this port invented and could not test.

*conv=noerror* cannot get past a block the device refuses outright. It recovers what a failed
read left in the buffer, and a refused transfer leaves nothing there, which is
indistinguishable from end of file - so *dd* stops at the first bad block whatever *noerror*
says.

*Files* counts one phantom partial record at each file boundary. It cannot arise at the default
of one file, and there is no tape here to raise it.

A number may carry trailing characters that are neither a multiplier nor a digit, and they are
ignored: *ibs=512junk* is 512.

*conv=swab* exchanges adjacent **bytes**, and says nothing about this machine's six-byte word.
There is no option that reverses a word, and none is proposed: the byte order within a word is
not a thing this machine's files disagree about.

**Note:** without a *conv*, *bs* copies each record whole and never looks at a byte; with one,
every byte passes through the conversion by itself. A bulk copy should therefore name *bs* and
no conversion, which is what makes it worth naming *bs* at all.
